        <body>
            <div xml:id="d791">

                <pb facs="0782" n="770" sameAs="frus:frus1937v01/pg770"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">711.00 Statement July 16, 1937/158:
                    Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Liberia</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Walton</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Monrovia</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1937-08-10T09:00:00-00:44">August 10, 1937—9
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received 1:50 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>39. My 38, August 6, 5 p.m.<anchor n="9" xml:id="d791-f1"/> Text of communication
                    from Liberian Government transmitted to me by Secretary of State Simpson reads
                    as follows. <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“The Government of Liberia has noted with satisfaction the statement made
                            on July 16th by the Secretary of State of the United States <pb
                                facs="0783" n="771" xml:id="pg771"/> of America with respect to
                            disturbed conditions in various parts of the world and the attitude of
                            the Government of the United States with regard to the international
                            problems arising thereout.</p>
                        <p>The Liberian Government has consistently been devoted to the ideal of
                            world peace as the basis of international security and the condition of
                            stability in international relations.</p>
                        <p>The statement of the Secretary of State merits cordial acceptance in all
                            parts of the world as the foundation of that international cooperation
                            without which the present uncertainties, inequalities and injustices
                            cannot be made to disappear.</p>
                        <p>This Government, whilst associating itself with the principles enunciated
                            by Mr. Secretary Hull, indulges the hope that the declaration thus made
                            by the Government of the United States of America will tend to stimulate
                            the international will to peace.”</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Walton</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
